# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
#
# RDR-BAR: the reader's own command bar, and the highlight it hands back
# (#329, #330) — open the log, open a page of an entry, come back to the
# log, and leave, with the screen the journal was opened over coming back
# whole.
#
# **What this leg is for.** Two things a person found on a real display
# and neither of which any test in this tree could have found. The bar
# read ` NEXT   PREV   EXIT` — indented one, spaced three — where every
# bar this game draws starts at column zero and puts one space between
# its commands (#329). And it was drawn in one call in the bright, so all
# three words were white end to end, where every bar this game draws
# paints the **initial white and the tail green** (#330). The second of
# those had a twin on the screen underneath: `Notes` on the party's own
# bar came back white end to end too.
#
# **A single leg, and it has to be one**, for `not-log-giveback.leg`'s
# reason: `N` reaches the program in a seam-off run, so a pair would be
# diffing the program's own answer to an unknown letter as well as the
# journal.
#
# **What it says instead is stronger than a pair could.** Since #330 the
# `Notes` splice hands the program's own bar highlight back on the way
# out — the routine sets the shared byte (`0x6B2B`, M5-E1g #304) to the
# group of the command it matched, and `Notes` is a group only because
# this seam put one there — so the frame after the journal closes is the
# frame before it opened, **on every pixel including the bar row**. The
# two legs that came before this one had to allow that row.
#
# **Driven, on the shorter boot** (`--code-wheel-answered`, #291), by
# hand, because the runner does not yet carry that flag — #293 owes every
# leg in this directory its re-drive. Same host, same disk copy, same
# `--wall none`, same dump cadence, same checks the runner makes.
# Measured off those stills:
#
#   * The reader's bar row, by character cell, on the listing and on a
#     full-screen page: `N` white at column 0 and `EXT` green at 1..3, a
#     space, `P` white at 5 and `REV` green at 6..8, a space, `E` white at
#     10 and `XIT` green at 11..13 — and on the page, the `1/2` label in
#     the same green at 20..22, six cells clear of the last word. Which is
#     the party's own bar's rule, measured on the same run at the same
#     cells: `A` white and `rea` green, `C`/`ast`, `V`/`iew`, `E`/`ncamp`,
#     `S`/`earch`, `L`/`ook`.
#   * `Notes`, on the party's own bar, after the journal has been used:
#     `N` white and `otes` green, the same as every word beside it. Before
#     #330 it was five white cells, because `--watch 6B2B` says the byte
#     was `01` on that bar after a load, `07` from the frame `N` was
#     pressed, and `07` still after the give-back. It is `01` again
#     thirteen frames after the press now, which is where the splice comes
#     out.
#   * One still, 9,625, catches `Notes` lit: the program's own routine
#     highlights the command it matched *before* it returns, and this
#     seam's point is the return. It is one dump of a screen the player is
#     leaving, and it is the same feedback the program gives for its own
#     commands.
#
about the reader's own bar, and the highlight the Notes splice hands back
kind single
program START.EXE
store tests/visual/reader-store.txt
cite-all
both code-wheel
# The boot #291 left: the seam on and the challenge already answered, so
# there is no wheel screen in this script and its frames are not the
# older legs' frames. The runner carries the flag as of this change, and
# this is the first leg to ask for it; #293 owes every leg before it the
# same re-drive.
code-wheel-answered
# No `document` line, and that is #290 rather than an omission: the
# code-wheel bypass was gated on a PDF of the wheel (#115) and no seam in
# this build is gated now. `camp-fix-exit.leg` is the same shape.
seam journal
until 300000000
every 25

# Slot A, the log, a page of the entry the log's cursor is on, back to
# the log by the bar's own `EXIT`, and out by it again. A screen this
# seam draws is painted over successive arrivals and is slow to appear —
# about 230 frames for a twenty-row page (#305) — so every range below
# starts well clear of the key that opened it.
key L@7550
key A@7800
key N@9600
key Return@10800
key E@12000
key E@13200

# Each screen, holding still with nothing pressed. Measured: the listing
# is one hash from 9,725 to 10,800, the page one from 11,050 to 12,000,
# and the listing again one from 12,100 to 13,200.
same 9800-10775
same 11100-11975
same 12200-13175

# The listing a page was opened from is the listing it comes back to,
# whole: the two ranges above are the **same** hash, not merely each
# steady.
equal 9800 12200

# And the screen the journal was opened over, back again after it —
# every pixel, with nothing excepted. It is byte for byte the pre-`N`
# frame from 13,425 to the end of the run (15,086 frames), which is what
# #330's give-back of the highlight bought: `not-log-giveback.leg` and
# `rdr-page.leg` both had to allow the bar row here.
#
# **And it is the line that catches the defect.** Driven once more with
# the give-back taken out and nothing else changed, this leg reports
# `frame 13500 is not frame 9575 again: 190 pixels differ, box
# 9,192,311,198` — the bar row and nothing but it. The three `same`
# ranges and the listing's own `equal` hold either way, because a
# highlight that never moves is a screen that holds perfectly still.
#
# **Five legs in this directory fail today and none of them is this
# one's doing**: `not-log-giveback`, `not-log-modal`, `not-page-back`,
# `not-page-modal` and `rdr-page` are the longer boot's, and they fail
# identically on the commit before this one — same frames, same pixel
# counts, same boxes. #293 is their re-drive, and the
# `code-wheel-answered` line above is the flag it needs.
equal 9575 13500
